# Design document

## Caveat Lector

Design documents are pits of despair, and this one is no different. The
information in it may be appallingly out-of-date, at best, and so do not look to
this document for Truth. It is a place to discuss ideas.

## Action Queue

A challenge UIs struggle with is to keep up with the user ("User") so that User
never experiences lag, hangs, or delays. This is especially tough when most of
what the UI does is interacting with -- at best -- an ABI call (as to mpv), or
at worst an API call to a remote server ("Server"). It gets even worse when the
remote server is over a laggy network; in the very worst cases, User is browsing
Server over a high-latency network, and Server is a slow Raspberry Pi from 2018
that User is also trying to run a Large LLM on at the same time.

The great Jeff Raskin [one of the pioneers in UX and designers of the original
Mac UI]{.marginnote} believed that CPUs should not sit idle, and that programs
should always be spending any free CPU cycles pre-fetching and pre-calculating
information so that User never experiences lag. Far be it for me to disagree
with Jeff, but there are situations when User doesn't want this to happen --
when it would, for example, saturate the LAN with API calls for data which is
never used, or bog down Server with supurious requests, or indeed defeat the
purpose of power saving strategies at play in User's computer. Jeff came up with
his idea in a different time, when CPUs didn't draw less power when they were
idling, and when making network calls was not as integral to designs.

### Algorithm, from 3,048m

Jeff was right, though: we _do_ want to minimize lag, for it is Annoying, and
Vexes User Unto No End. Action Queue attempts to address this, and it does so by
making some assumptions:

1. User is going to be jumping around alot, by scrolling through lists or some
   other actions, and that much of the information that they scroll _past_, they're
   not interested in.
2. Program should *not* eventually replicate the entire Server database in
   memory
3. Local caches are Hard: easy to implement, but hard to keep synchronized and
   therefore consistent.

{# One big queue, or a queue per page? For Server, one big queue would be
   better, as multiple queues could still swamp it #}
Action Queue -- hereafter `ActionQueue` -- is a structure that provides a finite
pipe of LIFO operations. As User performs UI actions, these `Actions` are pushed
into `ActionQueue`. `ActionQueue` has a background process which pulls things
off the queue and does them, with some caveats:

1. Unlike a Go channel, which is FIFO, ActionQueue pulls the most recently
   pushed thing and works on that -- in LIFO order.
2. ActionQueue's queue is both finite, and LRU-ish. That is, if more than the
   queue max size items are pushed onto the queue, the oldest ones get bumped off
   the list of things to do.
3. When ActionQueue completes a request, it calls a callback letting the caller
   know that _something_ has been fetched. If the _something_ is still relevant,
   the callback can do something with it; otherwise, the callback is discarded.
4. As long as there are things in the queue, ActionQueue continues merrily
   acting. This is a compromize that will hopefully pull enough information to make
   some information instantly available to User, under the assumption that if they
   requested it once, they might request it again.

### Pseudocode

{# Should we use a context to time-out in-progress API calls when new requests
   come in? #}

```
	globalActionQueue := new ActionQueue(size)
	list.SetChangeFunc(func() {
		show-loading-icon
		globalActionQueue.Queue( func() {
			// This could take a long time
			item := server.GetItem(id)
			put-item-in-cache
			if is-id-still-relevant {
				update-UI-with-item
			}
		})
	})
```

